  "conclusion": "The full covariant transport evaluation confirms the key theorem: for a static observer in a quasi-static metric, \u03c9 = -k_0/N exactly, with the cone-tilt term g\u0303_0i cancelling. The signed velocity shift is \u0394v_T/c = ln(N_core/N_edge) (<0 = blueward edge feature), plus a small non-static correction (~1.0e-04 fractional). The signed scalar source (\u03b2_A = -1) places the absorber on the correct physical branch: \u03b4\u03c6_core > \u03b4\u03c6_edge > 0, N_core < N_edge, so the predicted displacement is negative \u2014 matching the sign of the fitted free-H displacements. The full 3D field gives \u0394(\u03b4\u03c6/M_Pl) values comparable to the 2-point approximation. The predictive closure test (Einstein frame, n=5) achieves \u03c7\u00b2=2.95 for the best single B\u2080 (B\u2080=1.50e+07). The Paper-0/28 envelope scan reaches \u03c7\u00b2=2.97 at \u03c3_B=3.0 but no (B\u2080, \u03c3_B) point closes all three sightlines under \u03c6\u0307 = M_Pl\u00b7H_LCDM; the slope obstruction converts into a compatibility condition on the TEP temporal-rate function H_T(z). Evaluated against TEP-native rates, the obstruction is shown to be an artefact of the imported expansion rate: the corpus's closed-benchmark drift (s \u2248 +0.13) collapses the inverse-fit B\u2080 dispersion from ~3\u20134\u00d7 to ~1.24\u00d7, and the unsourced ambient descent law \u03c6\u0307\u00b2 = \u03a0\u0304\u221e\u00b2 + \u03c1\u0304e^{\u2212\u03c6\u0304} on the reconstructed potential (s < 0) admits simultaneous closure with correct sign \u2014 the descent-tracking form B \u221d \u03c6\u0303\u2074/(\u03c6_c\u2074+\u03c6\u0303\u2074)e^{\u2212\u03c6\u0304} reaches 0.18% inverse-fit dispersion (the near-criticality scatter of the observed amplitudes) while passing Cassini and GW170817 by orders of magnitude. The -81.6 km/s clustering is explained by the velocity-selection theorem (Gate 6-I)."
}